window.docContents[45].resolve({"/Type-systems/Operator-members/#Core-C______--Type-systems--Operator-members":{"contents":"An infix operator whose left operand is an object, and the indexing of an object, are the calls of members. The left operand decides, so no operator on int or bool changes meaning, and && and || are not overloaded.The type checker rewrites both forms into the call of the member, and from there they are ordinary method calls, with the visibility, the overload resolution and the dispatch of a method. The trace shows the rule MethodCall under the infix form, which is the point.\n\nA member may return . Every return of its body is then over an expression that denotes a location, checked by a walk that does not enter the body of a lambda, because the return of a lambda is the lambda's.The annotation rewrites the return e of such a member as return &e, the location as a value, an expression no program writes.In value position the same call reads . A location a member returns is that of a field or of an element of a vector of the receiver, and it survives the call because the object lives in . A member that returned the location of a parameter by value would return a freed location, and reading it is error, by the general rule of locations outside .\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Type systems","header":"7.2. Operator members","id":"/Type-systems/Operator-members/#Core-C______--Type-systems--Operator-members"}});